+   entry .Ref   uma célula de referência atômica, sobre `AtomicReference`

=   entry .Ref   an atomic reference cell, over `AtomicReference`
-     detail
+     detail

-         `Atomic.Ref(initial)` holds any value; `Atomic.Ref.vacant[value]` is an
-         `Atomic.Ref[Optional[value]]` which starts `Unset`. Reads never need `nn`, and a vacant
-         cell reads as `Unset` rather than throwing. The transitions `ere` and `since` take a
-         lambda literal, which may call a method on the value, construct from it, or match on it
-         and rebuild; a transition which is a function *value* rather than a literal goes
-         through `revise`. Where `value` is itself a function type, the value overload of `ere`
-         cannot be selected — the compiler commits to the transition overload — so store a
-         function with `cell() = f`.
+         `Atomic.Ref(initial)` contém qualquer valor; `Atomic.Ref.vacant[value]` é uma
+         `Atomic.Ref[Optional[value]]` que começa `Unset`. As leituras nunca precisam de `nn`, e
+         uma célula vazia é lida como `Unset` em vez de lançar exceção. As transições `ere` e
+         `since` recebem uma lambda literal, que pode chamar um método sobre o valor, construir a
+         partir dele ou casar sobre ele e reconstruí-lo; uma transição que é um *valor* de função,
+         e não um literal, passa por `revise`. Quando `value` é ele próprio um tipo de função, a
+         sobrecarga de valor de `ere` não pode ser selecionada — o compilador se compromete com a
+         sobrecarga de transição —, então armazene uma função com `cell() = f`.

=         function with `cell() = f`.
=
-         ```scala
+         ```scala

=         function with `cell() = f`.
=
=         ```scala
-         import soundness.*
+         import soundness.*

-         val latest: Atomic.Ref[Optional[Text]] = Atomic.Ref.vacant[Text]
+         val latest: Atomic.Ref[Optional[Text]] = Atomic.Ref.vacant[Text]

-         latest()                                // Unset
+         latest()                                // Unset

-         latest() = t"first"
+         latest() = t"first"

-         latest.since { current => current.let(_ + t"!") }   // t"first!"
+         latest.since { current => current.let(_ + t"!") }   // t"first!"

=         latest.since { current => current.let(_ + t"!") }   // t"first!"
-         ```
+         ```

-     entry .apply   `Atomic.Ref(initial)` makes a cell holding `initial`; `cell()` reads one
-         Two overloads which share the name: the companion's constructor over a fresh
-         `AtomicReference`, and the extension `cell()`, a volatile read which needs no `nn` and
-         does not throw on a cell holding `Unset`.
-     entry .vacant   `Atomic.Ref.vacant[value]`: an initially-absent `Ref[Optional[value]]`
-     entry .update   `cell() = value`: a volatile store to an `Atomic.Ref`
-     entry .publish   `cell.publish(value)`: a release store (`lazySet`) to an `Atomic.Ref`
-     entry .swap   `cell.swap(value)`: stores a value in an `Atomic.Ref` and returns the one displaced
-     entry .replace   `cell.replace(expected, updated)`: compare-and-set by reference equality, true if it took
-     entry .ere   applies a transition to an `Atomic.Ref` (or stores a value) and returns the value displaced
-     entry .since   applies a transition to an `Atomic.Ref` and returns the value installed
-     entry .revise   applies a transition which is a function value, not a literal, and returns the value installed
-         The escape hatch for a transition whose shape cannot be inspected — a function passed as
-         a parameter. It runs a compare-and-set loop, so the transition may be re-run under
-         contention and must be pure; a transition which returns the same reference settles
-         without writing.
